Subject: [PATCH 08023/11362] The win-state fix penalised correct predicates in
 three more places, and killed GOAL-REPAIR

Applies the review of the 2026-07-29 win-state work. Every finding was reproduced before being
acted on; two needed a different fix than the one proposed, and one of my own first attempts had a
bug that a pre-existing test caught. Line anchors below are POST-fix.

1. GOAL-REPAIR was dead, and the diff asserted the opposite (arc_llm_reinduction.py).
   Confirmed from source: at a level boundary `_previous_level_complete_grid` and `root_grid` are
   BYTE-IDENTICAL -- `_observe_level_boundary` computes
   `to_logical(grid_of(latest), detect_cell(grid_of(latest)))` and the caller immediately computes
   `self.root_grid` the same way from the same `latest`. So `_nonzero_count_predicate(exemplar)` was
   `count >= count` at the root: trivially true, hence rejected by the new root-true rule, hence
   `_repair_degenerate_goal` returned None on EVERY reachable call (verified directly). Worse,
   BEFORE the root-true rule it "succeeded" by handing the planner a goal already satisfied at the
   root -- a zero-action plan asserting the level was already won. It never worked.
   Fixed at the INPUT, not the gate: `_strictly_fuller_than_predicate`, the CONJUNCTION of "strictly
   more filled than the level ROOT" (non-degeneracy; False at the root, so the root-true rule stays
   exactly as strict) and "at least the EXEMPLAR's fill level" (which preserves the repair's ability
   to give up -- without it the bar is always the weakest possible).
   Two corrections to my own first attempt, both forced by evidence:
     - "filled" counts non-MODAL, not non-zero. ka59's background is colour 1, so every cell is
       non-zero and a count_nonzero bound is UNREACHABLE -- the repair would have declined on every
       real board, relocating the dead-repair rather than fixing it.
     - the reference is the ROOT (plus the exemplar bound), not the exemplar alone. Strict-vs-
       exemplar silently raises the bar from "reach the exemplar" to "exceed it" and broke three
       passing operator-directed tests (2026-06-25).
   The false "GOAL-REPAIR is not disabled" line is DELETED, not reworded.

2. The engine counterfactual is not oracle-distinct (arc_executable_world_model.py).
   Reproduced: an engine inventing colour 9 on the winning action, paired with a goal testing for
   colour 9 -- a colour in NO observed frame -- scores 0.5 graded on `next_grid` (veto FIRES) and 1.0
   on the counterfactual (veto PASSES). Engine and goal come from the SAME proposer in the SAME
   call, so mutual consistency is the expected confabulation mode.
   The review offered "gate on change_fidelity OR require the engine verified on that action". The
   fidelity floor ALONE does not restore the catch -- measured: a high-fidelity confabulator still
   passes. So both are required, and the per-action condition is the one that actually blocks it:
   corroboration from a real NON-level-up row on the same action, graded against REALITY (which is
   what keeps the gating decision independent even though the grading it authorises is not). Below
   either bar the row is UNGRADEABLE and dropped -- never quietly graded on `next_grid`, which is
   the penalise-correctness failure. Disclosed via
   `counterfactual_grading_is_not_oracle_distinct`, `n_levelups_ungradeable_low_engine_fidelity`
   and `engine_fidelity_used_for_counterfactual_decision`, surfaced onto the artifact row.

3. WorldModelVerifier.score graded level-up rows on the same wrong frame -- earlier and stricter.
   Reproduced: ONE identical, perfectly-honest engine scores accuracy 1.0000 / change_fidelity
   1.0000 on a window with no level-up and 0.6667 / 0.6667 on the same window whose last row is a
   real level-up. Since acceptance is `heldout_accuracy >= threshold`, that difference is the whole
   gate -- and it runs BEFORE either goal check, so fix 2's benefit was unreachable whenever the
   level-up landed in the held-out tail. No counterfactual exists here (the engine's prediction IS
   the counterfactual), so exclusion is the only sound option; `n_levelup_rows_excluded` records it.
   Direction stated plainly: this can only ADMIT engines previously rejected on rows carrying no
   information about them, never reject one that passed. A dishonest engine still scores 0.0.

4. Gate/planner budget inversion (arc_llm_reinduction.py). Confirmed the two loops counted different
   things under one name: `plan_in_model` increments BEFORE its shape check and dedup (raw engine
   calls); the gate incremented AFTER both (unique grids) -- ~11x more permissive than the search it
   guards. The gate now counts engine calls at the same position, reports `budget_unit`, and keeps
   `reachable_grids_evaluated` as the unique-grid diagnostic.

A BUG IN MY OWN FIX, caught by a pre-existing test rather than by me: dropping every level-up row
left `n == 0`, and `n_correct / max(1, n)` then yields 0.0 -- so the veto would fire HARDEST when it
has NO evidence, re-creating reject-correct-predicates through the denominator. An all-excluded
window now reads 1.0 (vacuously consistent) with `n == 0` as the discriminator, plus a
`consistency.n >= 1` guard at the veto site. An EMPTY INPUT keeps its historical 0.0 -- "you gave me
no transitions" and "your transitions were all ungradeable" are different claims.

PHASE-1 ENDPOINT CORRECTIONS (arc_actions_to_progress.py)

`hv_progress` was reporting an INSTRUMENT FLOOR as an observation. 4 of the 22 adaptered games
(cn04, ka59, sp80, su15) ship `hand_verifier=lambda _game, _frame=None: 0.0`, a literal constant,
+3 with no adapter = 7 of 25 immovable by construction; in the 24-cell retention A/B that was 8
cells (33%) pooled with real observations. Excluding them moves the headline correlation from
+0.0929 to -0.0040. `hv_progress` is now None when the verifier returned one value across two or
more distinct frames, via the extracted, directly-testable `hv_progress_measurable_from_stats`.
Verifier exceptions are COUNTED rather than swallowed (a None on the first call silently rebases
start_hv), and the 1000.0 sentinel fraction is recorded (a sentinel on the first call yields
hv_progress ~= 1.0 for free). ka59 -- the goal-gate control game -- is one of the constant stubs.

TESTS. Two new files (win-state/goal-gate 22 tests, hand-verifier measurability 8), 30 total. 7 mutations
applied one at a time, each killing its test; two of my first-draft tests did NOT kill their
mutation (`engine_calls <= cap` passes under post-dedup counting; a stats-only assertion passes when
the measurability rule is loosened to always-True) and were rewritten until they did. Three
pre-existing tests were updated, each with the reason recorded at the site: the `source` label (now
factually accurate), the live-veto fixture (extended with a corroborating row so it still asserts a
real mismatch under counterfactual grading), and its accuracy value (0.5 on a two-row window; the
load-bearing `< threshold` property is unchanged). The `pytest.skip` in the win-state file is
replaced by an assertion -- `objects_block` returns "" on internal failure, so a skip there would
silently disable the object-space check.

Failure set: 39 test files / 397 tests -- every file touching any changed symbol. AFTER 3 failures,
BEFORE (true HEAD) 3 failures, byte-identical reasons -- 0 new, 0 fixed. One of the three passes
unredirected (a CARNOT_ARC_E3_DIR artifact); of the two real ones, exp4350 is caused by the ka59
engine drift described below and exp4821 is a goal_energy wiring test, neither touched here.
The first BEFORE run was INVALID and discarded rather than reported: the venv's editable install made
the HEAD worktree import the MODIFIED tree, so it silently compared my code against itself and
"reproduced" 9 failures that were partly my own. PYTHONPATH precedence was verified explicitly before
the run that counted. This is the same class of error as the one the review caught in my Phase-1
controls, and it is why the number above is the second attempt.
Evidence dirs never written -- 148 checksums identical across results/arc_e3, arc_logo_snapshot,
arc_e3_origin_fixtures, before and after.

FULL SUITE: NOT COMPLETED, and I stopped it deliberately. It reached 60% over ~19 minutes with
hundreds of pre-existing failures and 1000+ skips, then I killed it by explicit PID. Two reasons, both
about value rather than convenience: (1) its result is NOT ATTRIBUTABLE without an equally long
matched HEAD run, and the repo-wide suite is heavily red at baseline, so a raw total would say
nothing about this change; (2) the run is itself destructive -- the polluted-file count grew 24 -> 36
while it ran, each file a tracked artifact I then had to restore. Continuing would have traded more
damage for no information. The failure-set comparison above is the attributable evidence and its scope
(every test file touching every changed symbol) is the correct one for a regression check. Separately,
the run emitted `Fatal Python error: Aborted` from a native/tqdm thread mid-run -- pre-existing
instability, unrelated, not investigated.

A CORRECTION OWED: PUBLISHED NUMBERS MOVED, AND HERE THEY ARE

`artifact-freshness-lint` refused the first commit attempt because 7 registered artifacts were built
by the pre-change modules. It was right to. All 7 were rebuilt (every rebuild offline -- no GPU, no
LLM; the three `experiment_60NN` ones read `results/arc_e3_origin_fixtures`, which is verified
byte-unchanged afterwards) and deep-diffed field-by-field. Timestamps, wall-times, git_head, mtimes
and checksums are excluded below as expected churn.

exp6011 (four-arm change-gate matrix) -- 208 substantive fields moved, ALL traceable to the level-up
row exclusion, and every one in the intended direction:
  * `n_transitions` 120 -> 119 across all 60 rows: each corpus contained exactly ONE level-up row.
  * `n_changing` 33 -> 32 (also 23->22, 28->27, 55->54): the excluded row was state-changing.
  * `legacy_accuracy` 0.725 -> 0.731092 (and 0.541667->0.546218, 0.766667->0.773109,
    0.808333->0.815126) -- purely the denominator change. NOTE: 0.725 is the canonical figure quoted
    in `arc_executable_world_model`'s own VerifyResult comment ("an engine can be wrong about every
    single mechanic and still read 0.725"). That prose cites the HISTORICAL lp85 measurement and is
    left as-is; the live artifact now reads 0.731092.
  * A VERDICT FLIPPED, and it flipped the right way: row 55 (game sp80, seed 1), arms
    `mask=0|gate=0|engine=ondisk` and `mask=0|gate=1|engine=ondisk`, went
    `nondegenerate: True -> False` with `reason` sharpening from `change_fidelity_below_threshold` to
    `degenerate_engine_no_correct_changed_cells`. Cause: that engine's ONLY `correct_changed_cells`
    (1 -> 0) and its entire `change_fidelity` (7e-06 -> 0.0) came from the level-up row's re-layout.
    A degenerate on-disk engine had been clearing the non-degeneracy check on a single accidental
    cell match against a frame it could not have predicted. It is now correctly called degenerate,
    and `change_fidelity` is exactly 0.0 -- which is what VerifyResult's own comment says an identity
    engine must score "by construction".
  * ALL 8 acceptance gates still pass and `honest_verdict` is unchanged
    (`complete_four_arm_matrix_measured`). No conclusion is overturned;
    `acceptance_gate_rejects_real_ondisk_degenerates` is better supported than before.

exp6013 (change-gate closure) -- 29 substantive fields, same cause, same direction:
  * `n_changing` 9 -> 8; `correct_changed_cells` 3885 -> 2342 (the re-layout row was contributing
    ~1543 accidentally-matching cells).
  * The spurious-writer separation IMPROVED: `attack_invented_rate` 0.777778 -> 0.875 and
    `min_over_spurious_writers` 0.777778 -> 0.857143 (higher = cleaner separation).
  * `change_fidelity` 0.996902 -> 0.996596.

exp6012, exp6021, and the three `outer_loop_*` artifacts: NO substantive scientific change --
provenance bookkeeping only (git_head, mtime, dirty_vs_head, build wall-times). One exception worth
naming rather than burying: `outer_loop_arc_generator_concurrency_fix`'s
`llm_off_recapture_18_cells_s` re-measured 59.488s -> 100.315s, and its `result` string with it. That
is a wall-clock re-measurement on a differently-loaded machine, not a changed finding, but it is a
published string that now reads differently.

NOT CHANGED, RECORDED

- `CARNOT_ARC_E3_DIR` is NOT a total redirect, and the comment claiming it resolves at call time was
  FALSE -- the expression runs once at import, so a process that imports first and sets the variable
  afterwards keeps writing to the real store. Two more writers bypass the constant entirely
  (`arc_e3_named_tail_gate._write_skill_file`, `arc_e3_fidelity_gate`, both `skill_*.json`). Comment
  corrected; the resolution order is not, because it would move where live code writes.
- `goal_exemplar_grading` (default-off) still demands True on an opening board, which the root-true
  rule rejects. Named at the site, not silently changed -- it is operator-directed.
- `_nonzero_count_predicate` left intact for its `propose_hierarchical_subgoals` consumer.
- `scripts/arc_gap_fill.py:52,63` carries the same win-state poison; untested script, unmeasured
  consequence.
- `ops/arc_solve_registry.yaml:4039` names ka59's `world_model_sha256: ec4c777b8f41...`; the file on
  disk is `f6f1da91...` and its predicate is `return False`. This is not inert: it is the direct
  cause of one of the two pre-existing failures.
  `test_experiment_4350_e3_explore_verify_plan_ka59.py:61` reads `wm.TARGET` off that engine, and
  the drifted file contains no `TARGET` at all (`grep -c TARGET` = 0), so the test dies with
  `AttributeError: module 'ka59_world_model_test' has no attribute 'TARGET'` -- identically at HEAD.
  A REQ-6035 last-write-wins casualty whose blast radius reaches the test suite. Not touched here
  (evidence, never-prune); it needs a restore-from-history decision, not a silent rewrite.

SEPARATE -- A KNOWN ISSUE CONFIRMED AND QUANTIFIED, NOT A NEW ONE. Running the suite rewrites
tracked result artifacts in place. I hit this, and then found it is ALREADY DOCUMENTED AND ALREADY
GATED: `.pre-commit-config.yaml:test-suite-mutation-gate` says so in its own comment (2026-07-29,
same day as this work), names the mechanism (`runpy.run_path` on the real
`scripts/experiments/experiment_NNNN_*.py`, which writes its artifact at the same fixed path the
historical one lives at), reports "one run left 39 tracked files modified", and cites as its example
"a hand-written corrigendum deleted from exp3946" -- the identical artifact I found damaged. So this
is a confirmation, not a discovery, and the credit for catching the class belongs to that hook.
What I can add is the FIELD-LEVEL breakdown, which the existing note does not give. Over 36 polluted
files from a run stopped at 60% (a LOWER BOUND; it was 24 files ten minutes earlier):

    flagged_adversarial stripped .............. 4 artifacts
    corrigendum_pending stripped .............. 4
    never-prune correction/restoration notes .. 5
    solve_provenance stripped ................. 1
    duration_s dropped or zeroed .............. 16

Each is load-bearing for a DIFFERENT discipline, which is the part worth recording: the damage is not
one field with one guard. The 4 `flagged_adversarial` artifacts (exp1938, exp2085, exp4162, exp4170)
are all four named in `scripts/determination_preservation_lint.py`'s docstring as part of the
2026-07-27 incident when the CONDUCTOR did this to seven artifacts -- so that lint would have refused
this commit, correctly. But `solve_provenance` has no equivalent commit-time guard, and its single
instance is the sharpest: `results/experiment_3946_r11l_first_solve.json` loses
`solve_provenance: "development_proxy"` and its note, which exist because that run reads engine
internals (`env._game.yfbjozweime`) for ground-truth pairs and is therefore NOT evidence of
live-agent self-discovery. Stripping it turns a declared development proxy back into an undeclared
solve -- the exact over-claim the ARC Live-Path Reachability provenance contract exists to prevent --
and the same rewrite drops the never-prune substrate-correction note and takes `duration_s` 0.2 to
0.0. The 16 dropped `duration_s` values matter for the same reason: it is the load-bearing
fabrication-detection signal.
Scale of the class: 282 test files under tests/python construct a `Path("results/...")` and write to
it. All 36 polluted files are restored byte-identically here (verified: empty diff against HEAD) and
no artifact content is committed.
Also, procedurally: take `scripts/test_suite_mutation_check.py --snapshot` BEFORE running the suite.
I did not, so `--check` correctly refused to guess and the pollution had to be identified by diffing
against the known session-start state instead. `--gate` passes on the restored tree.

